      "Buy-side liquidity (BSL) sits above old highs \u2014 this is where breakout buyers place their entry orders, and where short sellers place their stop losses. Sell-side liquidity (SSL) sits below old lows, the mirror image \u2014 breakout sellers entering, and long holders' stops resting below.",
      "Both represent large pools of opposing orders that a big institutional order can be filled against. This is exactly why price is so often drawn toward these areas before making its \"real\" move \u2014 not because the level itself has power, but because of what's genuinely resting there, waiting.",
      "Not every pool of liquidity is the same size, and learning to size them up correctly is a real skill. A single old high has some resting liquidity above it. A level that's been tested two or three times \u2014 equal highs or equal lows \u2014 has meaningfully more, because each touch that didn't break the level added another layer of stops and breakout orders at essentially the same price.",
      "A round number sitting near an old high or low compounds this further, for the same psychological reasons covered in the chapter on key levels \u2014 round numbers attract disproportionate order placement on their own, and when one lines up with a structural level, the combined pool is larger than either factor would produce alone.",
      "Reframe \"support and resistance\" as \"liquidity that price is being engineered toward.\" Institutional participants \u2014 banks, hedge funds, and the market makers standing between them \u2014 need real, substantial size to fill their orders without moving the market too far against themselves in the process. Genuine resting liquidity pools are exactly where that size exists.",
      "This reframing has real teeth behind it, not just theory. Regulators across the EU require CFD and forex brokers to publicly disclose what percentage of their retail client accounts lose money, and the European Securities and Markets Authority's own analysis across national regulators found that figure sits between 74% and 89%, with average losses per losing client ranging from roughly \u20ac1,600 to \u20ac29,000. That's not a marketing statistic \u2014 it's a mandated risk disclosure, and it's a direct consequence of retail participants consistently trading toward the obvious level instead of understanding what's actually resting beyond it.",
      "By the end of this chapter, you should stop asking \"will price go up or down\" and start asking \"where is the liquidity, and what's the most efficient path to it.\"",
      "That single mental shift is what the next part of this curriculum is built entirely around. Order blocks, fair value gaps, and liquidity sweeps are all, at their core, more precise tools for answering exactly that question \u2014 where is the liquidity, and how is price most likely to reach it."
