    "bodyHtml": "<p>An order block marks the footprint of institutional entry \u2014 specifically, the last candle in the opposite direction before a strong, displacing move away from it. The idea: a large order couldn't be filled all at once, so a portion of it remains unfilled at that origin candle, creating a zone price is statistically likely to return to before continuing.</p>\n\n<h3>The last opposing candle before displacement</h3>\n<p>\"Last candle before a big move\" isn't specific enough on its own \u2014 plenty of candles happen to sit before a big move by coincidence. What makes a candle a genuine order block is that it's the last candle moving <i>against</i> the direction of the impulsive move that follows, immediately before that move begins.</p>\n\n\n<div style=\"background:#1b1f26; border:1px solid #393e46; border-radius:12px; padding:24px 16px; margin:20px 0;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 460 180\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"460\" height=\"180\" style=\"width:100%; height:auto; display:block;\">\n  <line x1=\"80\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"80\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#5c6472\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n  <rect x=\"68\" y=\"128\" width=\"24\" height=\"18\" fill=\"#e5484d\" stroke=\"#f08488\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n  <text x=\"80\" y=\"170\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f08488\" font-size=\"10.5\" font-family=\"monospace\">last down candle</text>\n  <rect x=\"60\" y=\"120\" width=\"40\" height=\"35\" fill=\"#00adb5\" fill-opacity=\"0.15\" stroke=\"#00adb5\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\"/>\n\n  <line x1=\"150\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"#5c6472\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n  <rect x=\"138\" y=\"35\" width=\"24\" height=\"80\" fill=\"#03c988\" stroke=\"#4fe3ac\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n  <line x1=\"220\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"220\" y2=\"60\" stroke=\"#5c6472\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n  <rect x=\"208\" y=\"12\" width=\"24\" height=\"40\" fill=\"#03c988\" stroke=\"#4fe3ac\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n  <text x=\"185\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7fe0c2\" font-size=\"11\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\">displacement</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n\n<p>In an uptrend, that's the last down-close candle before a strong push higher. In a downtrend, it's the last up-close candle before a strong push lower. The candle itself often looks unremarkable \u2014 it's the move that follows it that retroactively makes it significant.</p>\n\n<h3>Rules for a valid order block</h3>\n<p>Not every candle before a move qualifies. This is the specific validity checklist that separates a real order block from a random candle that happened to precede one.</p>\n\n\n<div style=\"background:#1b1f26; border:1px solid #393e46; border-radius:12px; padding:24px 16px; margin:20px 0;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 480 150\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"480\" height=\"150\" style=\"width:100%; height:auto; display:block;\">\n  <g font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">\n    <rect x=\"10\" y=\"20\" width=\"140\" height=\"55\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#00adb5\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"80\" y=\"42\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#00adb5\">Genuine</text>\n    <text x=\"80\" y=\"58\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#00adb5\">displacement</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"170\" y=\"20\" width=\"140\" height=\"55\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#00adb5\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"240\" y=\"42\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#00adb5\">Leaves a</text>\n    <text x=\"240\" y=\"58\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#00adb5\">fair value gap</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"330\" y=\"20\" width=\"140\" height=\"55\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#03c988\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"400\" y=\"42\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#03c988\">Originates from</text>\n    <text x=\"400\" y=\"58\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#03c988\">real liquidity</text>\n  </g>\n  <text x=\"240\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#8b93a0\" font-size=\"11\" font-family=\"monospace\">all three, or it isn't a valid order block</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n\n<p>The move away needs genuine displacement \u2014 a decisive, larger-than-average move, not just an ordinary continuation candle. It should ideally leave a fair value gap in its wake, since that gap is direct evidence of the imbalance a normal continuation wouldn't produce. And it should originate from, or sweep, a relevant liquidity point \u2014 tying this concept directly back to the liquidity chapter earlier in this curriculum. An order block that satisfies all three carries real weight. One that satisfies only one or two is weaker evidence, not a hard rule to ignore entirely, but a reason to size the trade more conservatively or wait for additional confirmation.</p>\n\n<h3>Mitigated vs. unmitigated blocks</h3>\n<p>An order block price has already returned to and reacted from is mitigated \u2014 it's done its job once, and while it can still work again, its statistical edge is measurably reduced. An unmitigated block, one price hasn't yet returned to test, generally carries more weight.</p>\n\n\n<div style=\"background:#1b1f26; border:1px solid #393e46; border-radius:12px; padding:24px 16px; margin:20px 0;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 480 180\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"480\" height=\"180\" style=\"width:100%; height:auto; display:block;\">\n  <text x=\"110\" y=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#eeeeee\" font-size=\"12.5\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\">Unmitigated</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"60\" width=\"140\" height=\"30\" fill=\"#03c988\" fill-opacity=\"0.18\" stroke=\"#03c988\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n  <polyline points=\"30,150 60,120 90,100 120,75 150,50 180,30\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#eeeeee\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"110\" y=\"175\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7fe0c2\" font-size=\"10.5\" font-family=\"monospace\">never returned to \u2014 full weight</text>\n\n  <line x1=\"240\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"240\" y2=\"185\" stroke=\"#393e46\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n\n  <text x=\"360\" y=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#eeeeee\" font-size=\"12.5\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\">Mitigated</text>\n  <rect x=\"290\" y=\"90\" width=\"140\" height=\"30\" fill=\"#8b93a0\" fill-opacity=\"0.15\" stroke=\"#8b93a0\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n  <polyline points=\"280,150 310,110 340,95 370,105 400,85 430,50\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#eeeeee\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <circle cx=\"370\" cy=\"105\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#f5c542\"/>\n  <text x=\"360\" y=\"175\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#8b93a0\" font-size=\"10.5\" font-family=\"monospace\">already tested \u2014 reduced weight</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n\n<p>This distinction directly affects how much confidence you should place in a given block for a new trade. Two order blocks that look visually identical on a chart can represent very different quality setups depending entirely on whether price has already used one of them \u2014 a detail that's easy to overlook if you're only looking at shape and not history.</p>\n\n<h3>Putting it into practice</h3>\n<p>Order blocks are one of the more subjective concepts in this curriculum to apply consistently, precisely because the checklist involves several conditions rather than one clean objective rule. The fix is the same one from earlier chapters: apply the checklist mechanically, every time, rather than eyeballing a candle and deciding after the fact that it \"looks like\" an order block because you already wanted to take the trade.</p>",
